---
import MapQuizLayout from '../../layouts/MapQuizLayout.astro';

// Counts and facts come from scripts/build-map-quiz.mjs (world-countries,
// Natural Earth 1:50m, World Bank 2024 population).
const faqs: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['How many countries are in Oceania?', 'Fourteen: Australia, Fiji, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands, Micronesia, Nauru, New Zealand, Palau, Papua New Guinea, Samoa, the Solomon Islands, Tonga, Tuvalu and Vanuatu. The Oceania map quiz asks for all of them.'],
  ['What is the smallest country in Oceania?', 'Nauru, at about 21 km², followed by Tuvalu at about 26 km². After Vatican City and Monaco they are the two smallest countries in the world, too small to show as outlines at this scale, so the quiz marks each one with a tap ring.'],
  ['Are New Caledonia and French Polynesia in the quiz?', 'They are drawn on the map but not asked, because they belong to France. The same goes for Guam, which is a US territory, and other dependent territories in the Pacific.'],
  ['Is Australia a country or a continent?', 'Both. Australia is a country, and the continent it sits on is also called Australia. This Oceania map quiz uses the wider region of Oceania, which adds New Zealand and the Pacific island nations.'],
  ['Is the Oceania map quiz free?', 'Yes. Click, Type and Learn modes are free with no account, and your best accuracy and time are saved in your browser.'],
  ['Can I print a map of Oceania?', 'Yes. The free worksheet numbers all 14 countries, with blank lines for the names and a separate answer key.'],
];
const howTo: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['Read the name', 'The bar above the map names one country of Oceania at a time.'],
  ['Click it', 'Click or tap it on the map; the small island nations have tap rings.'],
  ['Learn from misses', 'A wrong click shows which country you hit; three misses reveal the answer.'],
  ['Finish all 14', 'Work across the Pacific and check your accuracy and time.'],
];
---

<MapQuizLayout
  region="oceania"
  title="Oceania Map Quiz – All 14 Countries of the Pacific, Free"
  description="Find Australia, New Zealand and all 12 Pacific island nations on our free Oceania map quiz. Tap rings for the tiny islands, type mode and a printable map."
  h1="Oceania Map Quiz"
  sub="Click the country we name — Australia to Tuvalu, all 14 countries, <strong>free printable worksheet</strong>."
  faqs={faqs}
  gameDescription="A free Oceania map quiz covering all 14 countries of Australia, New Zealand and the Pacific islands, with click, type and learn modes."
  howTo={howTo}
>
  <h2>How the Oceania Map Quiz Works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Oceania map quiz names a country, and you click it on the map.</strong> Found first
    time, it turns green; found after a miss, amber. After three misses the quiz shows you where it
    is and moves on, and each wrong click tells you which country you hit, so you learn the
    neighbours as you go.
  </p>
  <p>
    Oceania is mostly ocean, so this map is different from the others. Australia, Papua New Guinea
    and New Zealand are easy targets, but most of the other countries are scatters of small islands
    across thousands of kilometres of sea. The Oceania map quiz gives each of them a dashed tap ring,
    and you can zoom with the buttons, the scroll wheel or a pinch to see their real shapes. Type
    mode asks you to name a highlighted country, and Learn mode shows the capital, area and
    population of anything you tap.
  </p>

  <h2>How Many Countries Are in Oceania?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Oceania has 14 independent countries, and the Oceania map quiz asks for every one.</strong>
    Two of them, Australia and New Zealand, account for most of the land; the other twelve are
    island nations in the Pacific. Together the 14 cover about 8.5 million km², but they have only
    about 46 million people, fewer than many single countries elsewhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The map also shows places that belong to other countries. New Caledonia and French Polynesia are
    part of France, Guam is a US territory, and the Cook Islands are self-governing in free
    association with New Zealand. They are drawn but not asked. Papua New Guinea’s neighbour on the
    island of New Guinea is Indonesia, which is in our <a href="/map-quiz/asia/">Asia map quiz</a>
    along with Timor-Leste.
  </p>

  <h2>Oceania’s Four Groups</h2>
  <p>
    The Pacific islands are traditionally split into three groups whose Greek names describe them,
    plus Australia and New Zealand. Learn mode shows each country’s group.
  </p>
  <h3>Australia and New Zealand (2)</h3>
  <p>
    Australia is the sixth-largest country in the world and makes up about 90% of the land in the
    Oceania map quiz. New Zealand lies about 2,000 km to its south-east across the Tasman Sea, on two
    main islands, the North Island and the South Island.
  </p>
  <h3>Melanesia (4)</h3>
  <p>
    Fiji, Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu: the “black islands”, north-east of
    Australia. Papua New Guinea is the giant of the group, with about 10.6 million people and more than
    800 languages, and it shares the only land border in the Oceania map quiz, with Indonesia.
  </p>
  <h3>Micronesia (5)</h3>
  <p>
    Kiribati, the Marshall Islands, Micronesia, Nauru and Palau: the “small islands”, north of the
    equator or close to it. Note that Micronesia is both the name of the region and of one country,
    officially the Federated States of Micronesia. Type mode accepts “Federated States of
    Micronesia” and “FSM”.
  </p>
  <h3>Polynesia (3)</h3>
  <p>
    Samoa, Tonga and Tuvalu: the “many islands”, in the central and southern Pacific. New Zealand is
    Polynesian by culture too, but the quiz groups it with Australia, as the UN does.
  </p>

  <h2>The Smallest Countries in the Oceania Map Quiz</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Three of the ten smallest countries in the world are in Oceania: Nauru, Tuvalu and the
    Marshall Islands.</strong> These five are the ones that cost most players points:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nauru (about 21 km²).</strong> A single island just south of the equator, with no
      official capital. It is the smallest republic in the world and too small to draw at this scale,
      so the Oceania map quiz marks it with a ring.</li>
    <li><strong>Tuvalu (about 26 km²).</strong> Nine small islands north of Fiji, also shown as a
      ring.</li>
    <li><strong>The Marshall Islands (about 181 km²).</strong> Twenty-nine atolls and five islands,
      north of Kiribati.</li>
    <li><strong>Palau (about 459 km²).</strong> East of the Philippines, at the western edge of the
      Pacific island nations.</li>
    <li><strong>Micronesia (about 702 km²).</strong> More than 600 islands in four states, strung
      out east to west between Palau and the Marshall Islands.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tonga and Kiribati are also under 1,000 km² of land. Kiribati is the strangest shape on the map:
    its three island groups are spread across more than 3 million km² of ocean, and it is the only
    country in all four hemispheres. Its tap ring sits on the capital, Tarawa, in the west, but a click on any of its islands counts
    in the Oceania map quiz.
  </p>

  <h2>The International Date Line</h2>
  <p>
    The 180° meridian runs through the middle of the Oceania map quiz, and the date line bends around
    the countries near it. Fiji straddles the meridian itself. Samoa moved from the east side of the
    date line to the west in December 2011, skipping 30 December entirely, so that its calendar
    matched Australia and New Zealand. And because Kiribati moved its eastern islands to the same
    side in 1995, Kiritimati in the Line Islands is one of the first places on Earth to start each
    new day.
  </p>

  <h2>Memory Tricks for the Map of Oceania</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Use Australia as the clock face.</strong> Papua New Guinea is north, the Solomon
      Islands and Vanuatu north-east, Fiji further east, and New Zealand south-east.</li>
    <li><strong>Draw a line along the equator.</strong> Palau, Micronesia and the Marshall Islands are
      north of it; Nauru and Kiribati sit almost on it; everything else is south.</li>
    <li><strong>Pair Samoa and Tonga.</strong> They are neighbours east of Fiji, with Samoa to the
      north and Tonga to the south.</li>
    <li><strong>Find Tuvalu between Kiribati and Fiji.</strong> It sits roughly halfway between them,
      which is the fastest way to place a country you cannot see.</li>
    <li><strong>Zoom before you click.</strong> In the Oceania map quiz, rings that sit close together
      can overlap at full view. Zoom in on an island group until the rings separate, then click.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Oceania, Australasia or Australia?</h2>
  <p>
    Three names get used for this part of the world, and they do not mean the same thing.
    <strong>Australia</strong> is a country and also the name of the continent it sits on, which some
    definitions extend to New Guinea and Tasmania because they share the same continental shelf.
    <strong>Australasia</strong> usually means Australia, New Zealand and New Guinea together.
    <strong>Oceania</strong> is the widest term: Australia, New Zealand and every Pacific island group
    from Palau to French Polynesia, the definition the UN uses for its regional statistics. If you are
    looking for an Australia and Oceania map quiz, this is it, with all 14 countries.
  </p>

  <h2>Largest Countries and Population</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Australia has more than half of Oceania’s people as well as most of its land.</strong>
    The three largest countries hold about 94% of the population in the quiz.
  </p>
  <table class="cmp">
    <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Largest by area</th><th>Most people (2024)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Australia — 7.69 million km²</td><td>Australia — 27.2 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Papua New Guinea — 463,000 km²</td><td>Papua New Guinea — 10.6 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>New Zealand — 270,000 km²</td><td>New Zealand — 5.3 million</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Solomon Islands — 28,900 km²</td><td>Fiji — 930,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Fiji — 18,300 km²</td><td>Solomon Islands — 820,000</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    At the other end, Nauru has about 12,000 people and Tuvalu about 10,000. You
    can test size and population gaps like these in
    <a href="/higher-or-lower/">Higher or Lower: Countries</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Capitals Worth Knowing</h2>
  <p>
    Learn mode in the Oceania map quiz lists every capital. Australia’s is Canberra, not Sydney or
    Melbourne, and New Zealand’s is Wellington, not Auckland. Palau moved its capital to Ngerulmud in
    2006, and Nauru has no official capital, though its government sits in the Yaren district. Some capitals
    are not on the island you might guess: Honiara is on Guadalcanal in the Solomon Islands, and Suva
    is on Viti Levu, the largest of Fiji’s islands. When you know them, try the <a href="/capitals/">capital cities game</a> on a globe.
  </p>

  <h2>Printable Oceania Map Worksheet</h2>
  <p>
    The free <a href="/assets/printables/oceania-worksheet.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oceania
    worksheet</a> numbers all 14 countries, with blank lines alongside and a separate
    <a href="/assets/printables/oceania-answers.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">answer key</a>.
    The island nations are numbered in the margin with leader lines, so even Nauru and Tuvalu get a
    clear label. It is laid out for A4 landscape and prints fine on US Letter, and it makes a good
    check after a few runs of the Oceania map quiz.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="after">
    <h2>More Map Quizzes</h2>
    <p>
      Next door is the <a href="/map-quiz/asia/">Asia map quiz</a>, or try all 193 countries in the
      <a href="/map-quiz/">world map quiz</a>. To learn the outlines of Pacific nations one at a time,
      play <a href="/silhouette/">Globle Shapes</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="byline">
      Written by the Globle Unlimited team. Country list and sizes from the open world-countries
      dataset; outlines from Natural Earth (1:50m); populations from the World Bank (2024).
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</MapQuizLayout>
